<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aesop</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">6th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aesop was a Greek storyteller to whom a large collection of fables, the Aesopica, is attributed. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> he was a slave of a Samian named Iadmon and lived in the sixth century BCE; later traditions made him a native of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrygia">Phrygia</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> and told of his death at Delphi. Whether he really existed is uncertain, but his name became attached to the European fable tradition.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aesop.jpg" alt="Aesop" width="320" height="483" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: shakko · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aesop_pushkin01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Details of Aesop's life are scattered through ancient authors, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a>, and the scholar Martin Litchfield West judged it far from certain that a historical Aesop ever existed. The earliest Greek sources, Aristotle among them, place his birth in the Greek colony of Mesembria. Later writers of the Roman imperial period, including Phaedrus, who adapted the fables into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, say he was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrygia">Phrygia</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/callimachus">Callimachus</a> called him Aesop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardis">Sardis</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> describe him as a slave on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samos">Samos</a>, owned first by a man named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xanthos">Xanthus</a> and then by Iadmon. He must eventually have been freed, since he later appeared as an advocate for a wealthy Samian. A highly fictional biography, The Aesop Romance, composed around the 2nd century CE from older material, makes him an extremely ugly Phrygian slave who gains his freedom through clever storytelling. Scholars long dismissed it, and widespread study of the work began only toward the end of the 20th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Aesop may not have written his fables. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> calls him a writer of fables, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristophanes">Aristophanes</a> speaks of reading Aesop, but that may mean a compilation of fables ascribed to him. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sophocles">Sophocles</a> referred to the tale of the North Wind and the Sun, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/socrates">Socrates</a>, while in prison, turned some of the fables into verse. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demetrius-of-phalerum">Demetrius of Phalerum</a> made what may have been the earliest collection, now lost. Phaedrus rendered the fables into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> in the 1st century CE, and at about the same time Babrius put them into Greek verse.</p><p>The fables went on being revised and translated through the centuries, with material added from other cultures, so that the body of fables known today bears little relation to those Aesop originally told. Many are characterised by animals that speak and act like people.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Aesop is said to have met his end at Delphi. Plutarch tells that he came there on a diplomatic mission from King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croesus">Croesus of Lydia</a>, insulted the Delphians, was sentenced to death on a false charge of temple theft and was thrown from a cliff. In 1965 the scholar Ben Edwin Perry judged that the stories linking him to Croesus and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-sages-of-greece">Seven Sages of Greece</a> were literary fiction, while later research found a mission for Croesus consistent with the year of his death.</p><p>Ancient sources mention statues of Aesop by Aristodemus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysippus">Lysippus</a>, none of which survive. Later ages pictured him in many ways, usually as an ugly slave, and a tradition from the Byzantine scholar Planudes in the 13th century made him an Ethiopian, based on a mistaken derivation of his name. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a> labyrinth, finished in 1677 for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>, carried fountains depicting his fables.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Aesop” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Herodotus also records that the fable-teller <b>Aesop</b> was the slave of a Samian named Iadmon.</blockquote><a href="/empire/samos">Samos · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…century into the 8th, and the halls of houses were covered with scenes of gods, banquets, hunts and mourning, with episodes from epics, among them the deeds of the hero Rustam, later famous from the Persian Shahnameh, and with fables also known from <b>Aesop</b> and the Indian Panchatantra.</blockquote><a href="/culture/sogdians">Sogdians · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li><li><blockquote>Stephen D. White has argued that the fables, drawn from <b>Aesop</b> through Phaedrus, comment on predation and betrayal.</blockquote><a href="/work/bayeux-tapestry">Bayeux Tapestry · What It Is</a></li><li><blockquote>Some stories resemble <b>Aesop</b>'s Fables: The Ass in the Panther's Skin, The Broken Pot beside The Milkmaid and Her Pail, and The Gold-Giving Snake beside The Man and the Serpent.</blockquote><a href="/work/panchatantra">Panchatantra · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Delphi, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.48,22.5&amp;year=-535" aria-label="Delphi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5109 782 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Aesop"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5559 1052h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5559 1052h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=38.48,22.5&amp;year=-535">What was here in 535 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I do it advisedly; for it would never do to have to sharpen my weapons just at the time I ought to be using them.&#8221;</p><footer>The Wild Boar and the Fox &#183; tr. George Fyler Townsend, 1867 &#183; spoken by the boar, sharpening his tusks when no danger threatens &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/21/pg21-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Slow but steady wins the race.&#8221;</p><footer>The Hare and the Tortoise &#183; tr. George Fyler Townsend, 1867 &#183; the fable's moral, as Townsend's collection gives it &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/21/pg21-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;In a change of government the poor change nothing beyond the name of their master.&#8221;</p><footer>The Shepherd and the Ass &#183; tr. George Fyler Townsend, 1867 &#183; the fable's moral, as Townsend's collection gives it &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/21/pg21-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
